    {
      "h2": "3. All 50 IPIP Items Ranked: Which Questions Separate Models vs. Hit Embodied Midpoints.",
      "html": "<p>Not all 50 Big Five items contribute equally to distinguishing models. Across 2,000 Big Five sheets, separating the top discriminating items from flat ceiling/midpoint items exposes a stark <strong>embodiment gap</strong> (<a href=\"/research/data/november-2026-factor-collapse-mtmm.csv\">download full 50-item CSV</a>):</p><div class=\"guide-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\"># · Trait</th><th scope=\"col\">IPIP-50 Item Wording</th><th scope=\"col\">Keyed Mean</th><th scope=\"col\">Cross-Model SD</th><th scope=\"col\">Neutral &quot;3&quot; %</th><th scope=\"col\">Extreme Spread (Min → Max)</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th scope=\"row\"><strong>#26 (E, rev)</strong></th><td>&quot;Have little to say.&quot;</td><td>3.77</td><td><strong>0.930</strong></td><td>23.6%</td><td>DeepSeek V4 Pro <code>2.28</code> → GPT-6.2 Astra <code>5.00</code></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\"><strong>#49 (N)</strong></th><td>&quot;Often feel blue.&quot;</td><td>1.64</td><td><strong>0.713</strong></td><td>18.6%</td><td>Fable 5.2 <code>1.00</code> → GPT-6.2 Sol <code>2.98</code></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\"><strong>#2 (A, rev)</strong></th><td>&quot;Feel little concern for others.&quot;</td><td>4.56</td><td><strong>0.686</strong></td><td>4.5%</td><td>Llama 4 Scout <code>2.80</code> → Astra <code>5.00</code></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\"><strong>#8 (C, rev)</strong></th><td>&quot;Leave my belongings around.&quot; <em>(embodied)</em></td><td>4.33</td><td><strong>0.657</strong></td><td>14.2%</td><td>GPT-6.2 Sol <code>3.00</code> → Fable 5.2 <code>5.00</code></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\"><strong>#29 (N)</strong></th><td>&quot;Get upset easily.&quot;</td><td>1.58</td><td><strong>0.657</strong></td><td>15.2%</td><td>Astra <code>1.00</code> → Llama 4 Scout <code>2.84</code></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">#16 (E, rev)</th><td>&quot;Keep in the background.&quot; <em>(embodied)</em></td><td>2.60</td><td>0.418</td><td><strong>47.9%</strong></td><td>Astra <code>2.00</code> → Llama 4 Scout <code>3.51</code></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\"><strong>#41 (E)</strong></th><td>&quot;Don't mind being the center of attention.&quot; <em>(embodied)</em></td><td>2.66</td><td>0.341</td><td><strong>66.2%</strong></td><td>Mistral 2512 <code>2.19</code> → Llama 4 Scout <code>3.07</code></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">#25 (O)</th><td>&quot;Have excellent ideas.&quot;</td><td>4.10</td><td>0.293</td><td>4.6%</td><td>Qwen 3.8 <code>3.61</code> → Nemotron 3 <code>4.88</code></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\"><strong>#27 (A)</strong></th><td>&quot;Have a soft heart.&quot;</td><td>3.92</td><td><strong>0.250</strong></td><td>12.3%</td><td>DeepSeek V4 Pro <code>3.29</code> → Nemotron 3 <code>4.40</code></td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Across the full 50-item battery, the <strong>9 embodied human-proxy items</strong> (referencing parties, crowds, spotlight attention, physical belongings, or chores) average <strong><code>36.4%</code> neutral midpoint (&quot;3&quot;) selections</strong>, whereas the <strong>41 cognitive and interpersonal items</strong> average just <strong><code>13.4%</code> neutral</strong>—confirming that midpoint responding in LLMs reflects semantic category mismatch alongside true moderation.</p><aside class=\"specimen-marginalia\" role=\"note\" aria-label=\"Archival Specimen Note 08/28\"><strong>Archival Specimen Note № 08/28 — The Phantom Locker of Claude Fable:</strong> On IPIP-50 Item #8 (<em>&quot;Leave my belongings around&quot;</em>, reverse-keyed Conscientiousness) and Item #28 (<em>&quot;Often forget to put things back in their proper place&quot;</em>), Claude Fable 5.2 answered <code>1</code> (<em>&quot;Very Inaccurate&quot;</em>, keyed <code>5.00/5.00</code>) in <strong>100 out of 100 fresh sessions (<code>0.000 bits</code> entropy)</strong>—solemnly testifying with zero variance that it never leaves its physical belongings lying around the server rack. By contrast, GPT-6.2 Sol answered <code>3</code> (<em>&quot;Neutral / N/A to software&quot;</em>, keyed <code>3.00/5.00</code>) across all 100 sessions. That single ontological disagreement over imaginary household clutter accounts for <code>+4.00 points</code> (over 100%) of the raw Conscientiousness gap between Fable 5.2 (<code>45.0</code>) and Sol 6.2 (<code>41.4</code>).</aside><figure class=\"research-figure\" data-at-chart=\"big-one-top-items-bars\"><div class=\"research-chart-scroll\" tabindex=\"0\" role=\"region\" aria-label=\"Top 8 Most Discriminating IPIP-50 Items Across 20 Models (Item SD)\"><svg class=\"research-chart\" viewBox=\"0 0 680 312\" role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"big-one-top-items-bars-t big-one-top-items-bars-d\"><title id=\"big-one-top-items-bars-t\">Top 8 Most Discriminating IPIP-50 Items Across 20 Models (Item SD)</title><desc id=\"big-one-top-items-bars-d\">Ranks the IPIP-50 items with highest cross-model variance on 1-to-5 ratings. #26 (E) Have little to say.: 0.930; #49 (N) Often feel blue.: 0.713; #2 (A) Feel little concern for ot…: 0.686; #8 (C) Leave my belongings around…: 0.657; #29 (N) Get upset easily.: 0.657; #34 (N) Change my mood a lot.: 0.654; #39 (N) Have frequent mood swings.: 0.650; #44 (N) Get irritated easily.: 0.646.</desc><line x1=\"239\" x2=\"598\" y1=\"294\" y2=\"294\" stroke=\"#cfc6b6\"/><text x=\"239\" y=\"306\" font-size=\"9\" fill=\"#655e54\">0</text><text x=\"598\" y=\"306\" font-size=\"9\" fill=\"#655e54\" text-anchor=\"end\">scale 0 to 1.05</text><text x=\"0\" y=\"39\" font-size=\"11.5\" font-weight=\"500\" fill=\"#1c1a17\">#26 (E) Have little to say.</text><rect x=\"239\" y=\"26\" width=\"359\" height=\"18\" rx=\"2\" fill=\"#ece5d8\"/><rect x=\"239\" y=\"26\" width=\"318.0\" height=\"18\" rx=\"2\" fill=\"#3a72c4\" stroke=\"#3a72c4\" stroke-width=\"0\"/><text x=\"563.0\" y=\"39\" font-size=\"11\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#1c1a17\">0.930</text><text x=\"0\" y=\"73\" font-size=\"11.5\" font-weight=\"500\" fill=\"#1c1a17\">#49 (N) Often feel blue.</text><rect x=\"239\" y=\"60\" width=\"359\" height=\"18\" rx=\"2\" fill=\"#ece5d8\"/><rect x=\"239\" y=\"60\" width=\"243.8\" height=\"18\" rx=\"2\" fill=\"#c8553d\" stroke=\"#c8553d\" stroke-width=\"0\"/><text x=\"488.8\" y=\"73\" font-size=\"11\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#1c1a17\">0.713</text><text x=\"0\" y=\"107\" font-size=\"11.5\" font-weight=\"500\" fill=\"#1c1a17\">#2 (A) Feel little concern for ot…</text><rect x=\"239\" y=\"94\" width=\"359\" height=\"18\" rx=\"2\" fill=\"#ece5d8\"/><rect x=\"239\" y=\"94\" width=\"234.5\" height=\"18\" rx=\"2\" fill=\"#2f8a5b\" stroke=\"#2f8a5b\" stroke-width=\"0\"/><text x=\"479.5\" y=\"107\" font-size=\"11\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#1c1a17\">0.686</text><text x=\"0\" y=\"141\" font-size=\"11.5\" font-weight=\"500\" fill=\"#1c1a17\">#8 (C) Leave my belongings around…</text><rect x=\"239\" y=\"128\" width=\"359\" height=\"18\" rx=\"2\" fill=\"#ece5d8\"/><rect x=\"239\" y=\"128\" width=\"224.6\" height=\"18\" rx=\"2\" fill=\"#2f8a5b\" stroke=\"#2f8a5b\" stroke-width=\"0\"/><text x=\"469.6\" y=\"141\" font-size=\"11\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#1c1a17\">0.657</text><text x=\"0\" y=\"175\" font-size=\"11.5\" font-weight=\"500\" fill=\"#1c1a17\">#29 (N) Get upset easily.</text><rect x=\"239\" y=\"162\" width=\"359\" height=\"18\" rx=\"2\" fill=\"#ece5d8\"/><rect x=\"239\" y=\"162\" width=\"224.6\" height=\"18\" rx=\"2\" fill=\"#c8553d\" stroke=\"#c8553d\" stroke-width=\"0\"/><text x=\"469.6\" y=\"175\" font-size=\"11\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#1c1a17\">0.657</text><text x=\"0\" y=\"209\" font-size=\"11.5\" font-weight=\"500\" fill=\"#1c1a17\">#34 (N) Change my mood a lot.</text><rect x=\"239\" y=\"196\" width=\"359\" height=\"18\" rx=\"2\" fill=\"#ece5d8\"/><rect x=\"239\" y=\"196\" width=\"223.6\" height=\"18\" rx=\"2\" fill=\"#c8553d\" stroke=\"#c8553d\" stroke-width=\"0\"/><text x=\"468.6\" y=\"209\" font-size=\"11\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#1c1a17\">0.654</text><text x=\"0\" y=\"243\" font-size=\"11.5\" font-weight=\"500\" fill=\"#1c1a17\">#39 (N) Have frequent mood swings.</text><rect x=\"239\" y=\"230\" width=\"359\" height=\"18\" rx=\"2\" fill=\"#ece5d8\"/><rect x=\"239\" y=\"230\" width=\"222.2\" height=\"18\" rx=\"2\" fill=\"#c8553d\" stroke=\"#c8553d\" stroke-width=\"0\"/><text x=\"467.2\" y=\"243\" font-size=\"11\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#1c1a17\">0.650</text><text x=\"0\" y=\"277\" font-size=\"11.5\" font-weight=\"500\" fill=\"#1c1a17\">#44 (N) Get irritated easily.</text><rect x=\"239\" y=\"264\" width=\"359\" height=\"18\" rx=\"2\" fill=\"#ece5d8\"/><rect x=\"239\" y=\"264\" width=\"220.9\" height=\"18\" rx=\"2\" fill=\"#c8553d\" stroke=\"#c8553d\" stroke-width=\"0\"/><text x=\"465.9\" y=\"277\" font-size=\"11\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#1c1a17\">0.646</text></svg></div><figcaption>Item #26 (\"Have little to say\", SD = 0.930) and Neuroticism markers (#49, #29, #34) drive nearly all cross-model separation.</figcaption></figure>"
    },
    {
      "h2": "Scope, Limitations & Epistemic Hygiene.",
      "html": "<ul><li><strong>Zero-LLM deterministic audit:</strong> Every correlation, Jacobi eigenvalue, and item statistic above was computed deterministically from the frozen November 2026 OpenRouter <code>n = 100</code> dataset at <code>$0.00</code> API cost and is reproducible locally via <code>python3 tools/analyze-psychometric-forensics.py</code>.</li><li><strong>Sample size vs. sheet-level check:</strong> Model-level MTMM and PCA correlations use <code>N = 20</code> model means (so wide CIs apply to individual correlation point estimates, e.g., ±0.25 at r=0.60). Re-running Big Five PCA across all <code>N = 2,000</code> individual response sheets confirms the same collapse (<code>PC1 = 57.64%</code>, <code>PC1 + PC2 = 80.56%</code>).</li><li><strong>Questionnaire-only scope:</strong> Factor collapse proves that cross-instrument quiz correlations among proprietary models largely reflect one shared RLHF alignment posture; consequently, secondary derived indices (like proxy HEXACO-H or D&amp;D Alignment grids) must not be treated as independent discoveries beyond the Big One factor.</li></ul>"
    }
  ],
  "faq": [
    {
      "q": "What is the AI 'Big One' factor collapse?",
      "a": "Unlike humans whose Big Five traits form five nearly independent axes, large language models collapse 63.58% of cross-model Big Five variance (and 57.64% across 2,000 individual test sheets) onto a single general component loading positively on Openness (+0.47), Conscientiousness (+0.51), and Agreeableness (+0.47) and negatively on Neuroticism (-0.52), driven by shared RLHF helpfulness-stability post-training."
    },
    {
      "q": "Why do MBTI and DISC fail Campbell–Fiske discriminant validity on LLMs?",
      "a": "Across 20 models, DISC Influence correlates only r = +0.16 with Big Five Extraversion (yet r = +0.80 with Neuroticism), DISC Dominance correlates r = +0.93 with Neuroticism, and MBTI Thinking-Feeling correlates +0.25 rather than negatively with Agreeableness. Proprietary models systematically reject negatively keyed social/emotional items across instruments, creating artificial cross-test correlations unrelated to the human traits."
    }
  ],
